1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,557
This is STAT 331, video 13 a. The topic is multiple linear regression: the model, and what its coefficients mean.

2
00:00:10,007 --> 00:00:26,178
After this video, you can write the model in matrix form, and give the shape of each part. You can interpret each coefficient. And you can write the least squares criterion S of beta, and expand it. Video 13 b finds the estimate.

3
00:00:26,628 --> 00:00:42,436
Lecture 6 starts with a recap of three tools. We use all three in this video. First, matrix calculus. z equals a transpose y is one number. Its derivative with respect to the vector y is the vector a.

4
00:00:42,886 --> 00:00:53,425
For a quadratic form, z equals y transpose A y, the derivative is A y plus A transpose y. If A is symmetric, this is 2 A y.

5
00:00:53,875 --> 00:01:08,744
Second, random vectors. For a constant matrix A, the expected value of A y is A times the expected value of y. The variance of A y is A, times the variance of y, times A transpose.

6
00:01:09,194 --> 00:01:28,394
Third, the multivariate normal. Take z with independent standard normal entries. Then A z plus mu is multivariate normal, with mean mu and variance A A transpose. A linear function C y plus d of an M V N vector is also M V N.

7
00:01:28,844 --> 00:01:44,439
Here is the data example of Lecture 6. The file satisfaction dot csv has data on 46 hospital patients. The response is satisfaction with the quality of care. A higher value means more satisfied.

8
00:01:44,889 --> 00:01:55,534
There are three covariates. Age is in years. Severity is a score for the condition of the patient. Stress is the stress level that the patient reports.

9
00:01:55,984 --> 00:02:08,805
So each patient is one row. We have n equals 46 rows. The table shows the first 6 rows. We want to explain satisfaction with all three covariates at the same time.

10
00:02:09,255 --> 00:02:25,469
A pairs plot shows a scatter plot for each pair of variables. In row i and column j, the vertical axis is variable i and the horizontal axis is variable j. The diagonal gives the names and the ranges.

11
00:02:25,919 --> 00:02:42,644
The first row has satisfaction on the vertical axis. All three plots go down. The correlations are minus 0.79 with age, minus 0.60 with severity, and minus 0.64 with stress.

12
00:02:43,094 --> 00:02:55,297
The covariates are also correlated with each other. For example, severity and stress have a correlation of 0.67. Older patients also tend to have more stress.

13
00:02:55,747 --> 00:03:07,715
So when stress goes up, age also tends to go up. A plot of satisfaction against stress alone mixes the two effects. We need one model that contains all the covariates.

14
00:03:08,165 --> 00:03:22,607
Lecture 6 asks these questions. First: is mean satisfaction associated with stress, conditional on age and severity? In the model, this is a question about beta 3, the coefficient of stress.

15
00:03:23,057 --> 00:03:34,897
Second: how does mean satisfaction differ between older and younger patients, with the same severity and stress? This is a question about beta 1, the coefficient of age.

16
00:03:35,347 --> 00:03:43,582
Third: given the age, severity, and stress of a patient, can we predict the satisfaction? This uses the full equation.

17
00:03:44,032 --> 00:03:53,035
Later, we ask if the association with stress is different for older patients. That needs a new term in the model. Later lectures show it.

18
00:03:53,485 --> 00:04:07,586
Here is the multiple linear regression model. Observation i has the response y i, and p covariates, x i 1 to x i p. We stack the n responses into the vector y.

19
00:04:08,036 --> 00:04:16,377
The intercept beta 0 multiplies the number 1 in every row. So the first column of X is a column of ones.

20
00:04:16,827 --> 00:04:25,062
The next column holds covariate 1 for all n observations: x 1 1, x 2 1, to x n 1.

21
00:04:25,512 --> 00:04:36,392
We add one column for each covariate, up to covariate p. The result is the design matrix X. It has n rows and p plus 1 columns.

22
00:04:36,842 --> 00:04:49,749
The parameters beta 0 to beta p form the vector beta. The errors epsilon 1 to epsilon n form the vector epsilon. So y equals X beta plus epsilon.

23
00:04:50,199 --> 00:05:06,903
The errors are independent normal with variance sigma squared. In vector form, epsilon is M V N, with mean 0 and variance sigma squared times the identity. By linearity, y is M V N with mean X beta.

24
00:05:07,353 --> 00:05:20,537
Always check the shapes. y is n by 1. X is n by p plus 1. beta is p plus 1 by 1. So X beta is n by 1, the same as y and epsilon.

25
00:05:20,987 --> 00:05:35,643
Row i of X times beta gives beta 0, plus beta 1 x i 1, and so on, plus beta p x i p. So row i of the matrix equation is the model for observation i.

26
00:05:36,093 --> 00:05:44,669
For the satisfaction data, n is 46 and p is 3. So X is 46 by 4, and beta has 4 entries.

27
00:05:45,119 --> 00:05:52,329
The slides assume p plus 1 is less than n. We need more observations than unknown coefficients.

28
00:05:52,779 --> 00:06:00,929
With one covariate, the mean of y is a line. With two covariates, we need three axes: x 1, x 2, and y.

29
00:06:01,379 --> 00:06:06,904
Each observation is one point in this space. The points here are our own example.

30
00:06:07,354 --> 00:06:21,114
The mean of y is beta 0, plus beta 1 x 1, plus beta 2 x 2. This is a plane. With more covariates it is a flat surface in more dimensions, but the ideas are the same.

31
00:06:21,564 --> 00:06:32,316
The error epsilon i is the vertical distance from the point to the plane. Points above the plane have a positive error. Points below have a negative error.

32
00:06:32,766 --> 00:06:48,019
To interpret a parameter, isolate it. Set every covariate to 0. Then every term with a covariate is 0, and only beta 0 stays. So beta 0 is the mean outcome when all covariates are 0.

33
00:06:48,469 --> 00:07:04,000
This may not have a meaning. The data can be far from 0. In the satisfaction data, age 0 is not possible for an adult patient. Then beta 0 only places the plane. It is not interesting by itself.

34
00:07:04,450 --> 00:07:14,669
Now beta 1. Take a point with covariate values x 1, x 2, and so on. The mean outcome is the height of the plane above this point.

35
00:07:15,119 --> 00:07:24,740
Now increase x 1 by 1. Keep x 2 and all other covariates the same. The mean outcome is the height of the plane at the new point.

36
00:07:25,190 --> 00:07:31,739
The plane goes up by beta 1. This rise is the same at every point, because the plane is flat.

37
00:07:32,189 --> 00:07:49,597
Subtract the two means. beta 0 cancels. The terms with x 2 to x p cancel, because these values did not change. Only beta 1 times x 1 plus 1, minus beta 1 times x 1, stays. That is beta 1.

38
00:07:50,047 --> 00:08:02,826
In general, beta j is the difference in mean outcome for a one-unit change in covariate j, holding the other covariates fixed. Always say: holding the other covariates fixed.

39
00:08:03,276 --> 00:08:27,937
Here are the estimates for the satisfaction data. Lecture 6 page 37 computes them, and we got the same numbers from the data. Beta hat 1 for age is minus 1.1416. Beta hat 3 for stress is minus 13.4702. The green line is the fitted mean against age, at stress 2.3 and severity 50.

40
00:08:28,387 --> 00:08:43,150
Take two patients with the same severity and stress. One is 10 years older. The fitted mean satisfaction of the older patient is 11.4 lower. That is 10 times 1.1416.

41
00:08:43,600 --> 00:09:02,651
Now fix age and severity, and increase stress by 0.5. The line moves down by 0.5 times 13.47, which is 6.74. The lines are parallel. The age effect is the same at every stress level in this model.

42
00:09:03,101 --> 00:09:19,015
Added, not on the slides. A simple regression of satisfaction on stress alone gives a slope of minus 37.12. This slope also contains the effect of age and severity, because they go up with stress.

43
00:09:19,465 --> 00:09:31,924
The multiple regression slope is minus 13.47. It compares patients with the same age and severity. This is why we must say: holding the other covariates fixed.

44
00:09:32,374 --> 00:09:50,657
We do not know beta. We estimate it with least squares. For any candidate beta, y minus X beta is the vector of differences between the data and the candidate means. S of beta is this vector times itself. That is the sum of the squared differences.

45
00:09:51,107 --> 00:10:06,317
Here is a picture with our own small data set, with one covariate. Take the candidate beta 0 equals 3 and beta 1 equals 0.3. Each orange square has the area of one squared difference.

46
00:10:06,767 --> 00:10:18,031
S is a function of beta. On the right, each gray curve joins the values of beta with the same S. The candidate gives S equal to 5.55.

47
00:10:18,481 --> 00:10:34,716
The least squares estimate beta hat is the value of beta with the smallest S. Here it is 1.3 and 0.9, with S equal to 1.9. The squares are smaller. Next, we find beta hat with calculus.

48
00:10:35,166 --> 00:10:51,379
First, expand S of beta. The transpose of y minus X beta is y transpose, minus beta transpose X transpose. The reason is that the transpose of a product A B is B transpose A transpose.

49
00:10:51,829 --> 00:11:01,557
Multiply the two brackets. Each part of the first bracket multiplies each part of the second. This gives four terms. The grid shows them.

50
00:11:02,007 --> 00:11:14,786
Look at the term y transpose X beta. Its shape is 1 by n, times n by p plus 1, times p plus 1 by 1. So it is 1 by 1. It is one number.

51
00:11:15,236 --> 00:11:27,012
A number is equal to its transpose. The transpose of y transpose X beta is beta transpose X transpose y. So the two yellow terms are equal.

52
00:11:27,462 --> 00:11:38,918
Add the two middle terms. S of beta equals y transpose y, minus 2 y transpose X beta, plus beta transpose X transpose X beta.

53
00:11:39,368 --> 00:11:54,408
To recap. The model is y equals X beta plus epsilon. X has n rows and p plus 1 columns, with a first column of ones. Beta 0 is the mean outcome when all covariates are 0.

54
00:11:54,858 --> 00:12:03,583
Beta j is the difference in mean outcome for a one-unit change in covariate j, holding the other covariates fixed.

55
00:12:04,033 --> 00:12:13,676
Least squares minimizes S of beta. We expanded it into three terms. Video 13 b takes the derivative and finds beta hat.
